From 9c4deb163383a6d7e0b8fe20adcf43fe1034f0fb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheCryptoDonkey <TheCryptoDonkey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 08:05:26 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] feat: route linked identities' Nostr operations to their
 signer

identity.nostr-sign for a linked identity sends the full event to the
remote signer as sign_event and returns it only after checking it
against the request; a bare event_hash is refused. An event without
created_at is timestamped once, when the request first goes to the
signer. The four NIP-04 and NIP-44 methods go to the matching NIP-46
methods, and refusals come back as typed errors with a clear message.
publish-profile signs kind 0 through the signer too.

Signer sessions are kept open per linked identity, so requests share a
relay connection. Until the signer accepts the session's connect (sent
from the stored client key, with the stored secret if the URI had one),
each request first sends it, waits up to 5 s for a reply (any reply
ends the wait) and goes ahead either way; a request the signer serves
also marks the session accepted. Once accepted, a request answered as unauthorised (a
signer that restarted and forgot the client) gets one more connect and,
if that is accepted, one retry; a person's refusal is never retried. A
session closes after five idle minutes, after a transport failure (the
next request reconnects), when its identity is deleted or re-linked,
and when 32 are open and it is the least recently used.

Any verified reply from the signer, a refusal included, counts as it
being there. Before a request, unless the signer has replied within the
last 30 s, the session sends a NIP-46 ping, timed once the relays are
connected. Any reply, a refusal included, lets
the request through; no reply within 5 s fails it as offline without
sending it. While a request is out, no reply within 5 s sends a ping
too: silence to that fails the request as offline, while an answer
(the signer is there, likely waiting on a person) keeps the request
waiting and is repeated every 30 s. A signer that is gone behind a
working relay is so reported within seconds rather than after the 60 s
request timeout, and signers answer ping while an approval is pending,
so a person deciding is not cut off.

A reply is taken as soon as it arrives, without waiting for every relay
to acknowledge the request: a relay that is slow to answer, or that asks
for authentication first, no longer holds up a signer that has already
replied on another relay.

Remote requests run on their own task, so a dropped HTTP request cannot
cancel a prompt that is already on the device. A retry with the same
identity, method and request digest joins the pending request, and a
success keeps answering identical retries for 60 s before it is
dropped, so the dashboard's 15 s retry loop no longer raises extra
prompts. The digest is the event id, or for an event that arrived
without created_at, its pubkey, kind, tags and content. Failures,
panics included, are not cached, and at most 256 completed requests are
kept.

Anything that needs the Nostr secret of a linked identity refuses:
create-nostr-key, local hash signing and loading the keys. export-keys
omits the Nostr part and never exports the signer client key.
